In attempt to narrow the relevant sequence space for a protein to be searched experimentally, various computational methods have been developed. Computational methods may specifically aid in narrowing the sequence space of a protein by predicting the effect of mutations on protein folding and molecular recognition [7,15–18]. Such approaches have been successfully applied to the de novo design of novel catalytic activities for non-native retroaldol and Kemp elimination reactions [18,19]. Additionally, computational methods may also aid in predicting beneficial mutations via analysis of existing sequence– activity data derived from empirical directed evolution studies. One such approach developed by Fox and coworkers [20] reported the development of a statistical model (ProSAR) that describes the synergistic effects of mutations based on previously measured fitness values, which can be employed to design focused combinatorial libraries for evolution. This model was employed to improve the activity of a halohydrin dehalogenase by approximately 4000-fold, demonstrating the utility of such models [21]. Nevertheless, the computational methods that most accurately predict protein function based on sequence are those that incorporate experimental data, which are dependent on generation of sequenceto-activity libraries with high diversity.
## Introduction
## Current challenges in protein engineering
Directed evolution provides a valuable tool for engineering novel and improved protein functions for industrial use. Specifically, using conventional evolutionary strategies such as error-prone polymerase chain reaction, DNA shuffling, and site-saturation mutagenesis, enzymes with desired functions have been identified. For example, these methods have been successfully employed to evolve enzymes with improved thermostability and tolerance to pH changes, oxidation, organic solvents, and surfactants [1–4]. However, the size of the sequence
An alternative approach to evolving proteins is to systematically search the entire linear sequence space after which combinations of beneficial point mutations may be assembled. This approach, in addition to efficient screening and selection methods, requires effective strategies for creating comprehensive, rational libraries that are trackable in high-throughput. Importantly, for a single protein, the reduction of sequence space using this approach enables the use of traditional screening and selection methods. In this review, recent advances in synthetic biology that make the creation of such libraries tractable, facilitating accelerated evolution strategies for protein engineering are discussed. The creation of vast, trackable, and rational libraries may also expand the targets of protein engineering beyond the scope of single proteins. In theory, exploitation of these advances permits the engineering of entire gene clusters and metabolic pathways of industrial relevance with unprecedented speed and success.
## Solutions from multiplex recombineering Recombineering as a tool in protein engineering
As a method for producing genetic diversity and creating sequence-to-activity mapping libraries for protein engineering, recombineering presents considerable opportunities. Recombineering involves the use of linear (single or double-stranded) DNA oligonucleotides that contain regions of homology with a target gene to introduce point mutations, insertions, or deletions in chromosomal DNA (Figure 1a). Specifically, in recombineering, a segment of the target gene is replaced by the designed oligonucleotide via seamless insertion of the oligonucleotide between the homologous regions $\left[22,23^{\bullet},24-28\right]$ . The insertion of the oligonucleotide is typically facilitated by the use of Lambda Red, RecET, or other bacteriophage-based recombination systems in Escherichia coli [27–29]. Notably, while eliminating the need for in vitro cloning techniques, recombineering may be employed to create targeted sequence-to-activity mapping libraries, including comprehensive libraries for alanine scanning, single site saturation, and entire gene saturation mutagenesis with unparalleled genetic diversity. In this way, using recombineering for gene saturation mutagenesis, the effect of all possible single amino acid substitutions across the entire open reading frame of a protein may be mapped to a desired property. Beneficial mutations may subsequently be combined in a rational manner to efficiently explore the complete combinatorial mutation space using libraries that are synthetically tractable (Figure 1b). Furthermore, the efficiency with which the combinatorial space is explored may be, in theory, enhanced via combining the power of recombineering with search algorithms such as ProSAR [20].
The utility of recombineering to rationally design novel protein variants and explore vast combinatorial spaces represents a significant advantage over conventional protein engineering methods. This advantage was recently demonstrated using the recombineering-based technique Multiplex Automated Genome Engineering (MAGE). Using MAGE, the expression levels of 24 proteins in $E$ . coli were combinatorially optimized in parallel via the introduction of mutations in the ribosome binding sites of the corresponding genes [30]. MAGE, which was used to generate approximately ${10}^{9}$ genetic variants per day, was specifically employed to engineer lycopene biosynthesis. The resulting variants produced lycopene at higher titers than previously achieved by rational engineering approaches [31]. Additionally, using MAGE, the 20-base pair T7 promoter was introduced in front of multiple genes in parallel to optimize the production of aromatic amino acids $[32^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . Such strategies could also be readily extended to the parallel, combinatorial optimization of enzyme active sites or ligand binding surfaces. In this way, MAGE may have considerable utility to explore large, combinatorial sequence spaces of not only single proteins, but also multiple proteins, whole pathways, and potentially entire genomes.
While recombination has been developed primarily in $E$ . coli and yeast, expansion of these tools to other organisms will expand the utility of recombineering approaches for protein engineering. The use of homologous recombination in other organisms may permit the engineering of proteins that are difficult to express in bacteria or yeast or that require posttranslational modifications. A recent study utilized standard recombineering techniques in $E$ . coli to engineer fluorescent tags on an existing fosmid library of Caenorhabditis elegans genes $[33^{\bullet}]$ . The resulting library covered a large number of the open reading frames in $C$ . elegans, enabling the function of a wide variety of proteins to notably be probed in vivo in $C$ . elegans. These tools are also currently being expanded to other bacterial species, including Gram positive bacteria [34–36].
## Implications of advances in DNA synthesis and assembly for multiplex recombineering
Continual improvements in DNA synthesis and assembly have widespread implications in the use of recombineering for protein engineering. Specifically, as the cost of
