DNA decreases and the quantity of DNA that can be synthesized increases, larger sequence spaces may be explored by means of recombineering. Current microarray fabrication methods that take advantage of spatially directed synthesis and improved chemistry enable parallel synthesis of approximately $10^{4}$ to ${10}^{6}$ unique oligonucleotides [37–39]. Strategies for retrieval, amplification, and processing of array-containing oligonucleotides have also been established that enable the use of DNA microarrays for directed library generation $[40{-}42,43^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . As such, oligonucleotides can be tailored for rational targeting of multiple sites within promoters or open reading frames throughout the entire genome of an organism. Thus, while conventional methods for rational library generation have traditionally limited rational library size construction to $10^{1}$ to $10^{2}$ variants, parallel DNA synthesis and assembly methods in conjunction with recombineering facilitate the synthesis of large, targeted libraries on a laboratory timescale (i.e. days to weeks).
With the ability to synthesize large, rational libraries for protein engineering, global site saturation libraries may be constructed for a wide range of feasible targets. As mentioned previously, such targets may span the size of single proteins up to every gene in a modest-sized biosynthetic pathway. If modifications are limited to fewer sites such as those that comprise the active site of an enzyme or promoter regions, the number of feasible protein targets may be expanded to a genome-wide scale. The size of necessary libraries to map all possible single point mutations (i.e. linear mutation space) for a few residues up to every gene in the entire $E$ . coli genome is depicted in Table 1. For comparison, the size of the combinatorial mutation space, which remains synthetically intractable even for a single protein, is shown side-byside. The sheer size of the combinatorial space for even small targets highlights the need for efficient methods for traversing the mutation space of proteins. By limiting the combinatorial space to that of positive mutations found from searching the linear sequence space, the most relevant mutation landscapes may be directly explored.
Ultimately, the ability to optimize the function of pathways that require the concerted activities of many genes to be precisely tuned may have far reaching consequences in synthetic biology. For example, commercially valuable products such as biofuels and secondary metabolites are often produced via heterologous pathways in $E$ . coli due to the genetic tractability of this organism [44]. Optimization of such pathways can be a laborious process in which many iterations of directed evolution are required to address particular aspects of the pathway such as gene expression levels or catalytic activities of the relevant proteins. This notion is underscored in recent work involving the optimization of the production of artemisinin, an antimalarial precursor, in $E$ . coli [45]. Parallel optimization of such multi-genic systems using an approach similar to MAGE would enable rapid engineering of metabolic pathways or other complex genetic traits of interest, and allow for identification of genotypes associated with improved activities.
## Multiplex tracking of sequence-to-activity relationships via recombineering
In conventional directed evolution methods, beneficial mutations must be identified postselection via sequencing the entire gene. This step often represents a rate, and cost, limiting step in such methods due to the need to isolate and sequence many clones individually. Consequently, the overall throughput of evolutionary methods and, in turn, the resulting number of beneficial mutations that may be identified is usually of the order of only $10^{1}$ to $10^{2}$ . This limitation is particularly inhibitory in the simultaneous evolution of multiple proteins and whole pathways in parallel, where the number of genes to be sequenced may become increasingly large. Through recombineering, this bottleneck may be circumvented via incorporation of unique DNA barcodes into the design of oligonucleotides that are used to introduce the desired mutation. By barcoding each variant in the library, beneficial mutations may be rapidly identified via sequencing of short strands of DNA only. In this way, the use of barcoded libraries enables mutations to be identified more efficiently and cheaply than with conventional libraries. More broadly, using barcoded recombineering libraries in combination with high-throughput sequencing technologies may open new avenues for evolving multiple proteins and entire biosynthetic networks inside of cells with unmatched throughput. The creation of DNA barcoded libraries for recombineering has been facilitated by the development of novel algorithms for the design of large numbers of orthogonal DNA barcode sequences such as DeLOB [46].
The use of molecular barcoding in conjunction with recombineering has recently been established as a means to track genetic modifications in vivo. In one demonstration from the Gill group, Warner et al. $[43^{\bullet\bullet}]$ successfully implemented DNA barcodes via recombineering to map the simultaneous effect of thousands of mutations that modulate the expression of the entire $E$ . coli genome to growth under various stress conditions. Using this approach, named Trackable Recursive Multiplex Recombineering (TRMR), specific genes that confer tolerance to various toxic compounds such as $\upbeta$ -glucoside, D-fucose, methylglyoxal, and valine were rapidly identified $[43^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . As an extension of this work, Sandoval and co-workers $[47^{\bullet}]$ exploited the use of DNA barcoded libraries while also combining TRMR with a recursive, multiplex recombineering approach, similar to MAGE. The authors rapidly identified mutations in the $E$ . coli genome that permit growth under different stress conditions such as the presence of cellulosic hydrolysate. Additionally, although not for recombineering, barcoded DNA libraries have also previously been exploited in other organisms, including yeast, highlighting the potential utility of barcoded recombineering techniques for scanning the effect of mutations in proteins in alternative host systems [48,49].
## Conclusions
## New directions enabled by recombineering technology
Although still dependent on the use of appropriate screens or selections, advancements in recombineering enable the engineering of proteins with novel and improved functions. One area in which recombineering may have utility that is of marked interest in many communities is the evolution of enzyme activity and stability in non-natural solvent environments. Of particular interest to our group and others is enhancing the catalytic efficiency of cellulases in ionic liquids due to the remarkable miscibility of cellulose in many ionic liquids [50–54]. The long-term goal of such efforts is to exploit ionic liquids as solvents for clean and efficient biocatalytic hydrolysis of cellulose while eliminating the need for biomass pretreatment. Ultimately, this goal will necessitate the simultaneous engineering of multiple cellulase enzymes (i.e. endoglucanases, exogluncanases, $\upbeta$ -glucosidases) in parallel that are required for cellulose hydrolysis, which is possible via multiplex recombineering. Additionally, recombineering provides a novel avenue for evolving proteins with an expanded genetic code. Specifically, using recombineering, comprehensive sequence-to-activity mapping libraries that include the mutation of residues to non-natural amino acids — for which orthogonal aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase and tRNA have been developed — may be created. While also broadening the sequence search space, the inclusion of non-natural amino acids permits the sampling of enzyme variants with bioorthogonal chemical moieties, which have unique physicochemical properties. Such moieties may include, among others, ketone, aldehyde, azide, alkyne, and halogenated groups, which are non-existent in naturally occurring amino acids. Recombineeringbased approaches (i.e. MAGE) have previously been employed to engineer amber stop codons across the entire $E$ . coli genome [55], which provide the basis for nonnatural amino acid scanning mutagenesis.
